      "19184": {
        "comments": [
          {
            "attachment_id": {},
            "tags": [],
            "is_private": false,
            "time": "2026-09-26T01:44:50Z",
            "id": 103711,
            "creator": "kevinushey",
            "creation_time": "2026-09-26T01:44:50Z",
            "is_markdown": false,
            "count": 0,
            "text": "When an R error is raised from inside the Rd parser, parse_Rd() leaks\ntwo kinds of malloc()ed memory: the bison parser stack, once the input\nnests deeper than 200 levels (about 52 KB for 400 levels), and the\nlexer's pushback buffer, once macro expansion has grown it (128 KB for\na 20000-character macro body). Errors raised during the parse include\nthe \"Unexpected end of input (in \\\" quoted string ...)\" error, \"macros\nnested too deeply\", the user-macro argument errors, and any warning\nunder options(warn = 2).\n\nReproducible example:\n\n    deep <- paste0(strrep(\"\\\\emph{\", 400), \"\\\\code{\\\"abc\")\n    long <- paste0(\"\\\\newcommand{\\\\foo}{\", strrep(\"y\", 20000), \"}\",\n                   \"\\\\foo \\\\foo \\\\code{\\\"abc\")\n    tools::parse_Rd(textConnection(deep), fragment = TRUE)\n    ## Error in tools::parse_Rd(textConnection(deep), fragment = TRUE) :\n    ##   Unexpected end of input (in \" quoted string opened at <connection>:1:2408)\n    for (i in 1:500) {\n        try(tools::parse_Rd(textConnection(deep), fragment = TRUE), silent = TRUE)\n        try(tools::parse_Rd(textConnection(long), fragment = TRUE), silent = TRUE)\n    }\n\nObserved: each failing call leaks the grown stack or pushback buffer,\nabout 97 MB over the loop. Expected: the error is signalled and nothing\nis retained.\n\nAnalysis (src/library/tools/src/gramRd.y, trunk r90590):\n\n- yyparse(), as generated by bison, keeps its stacks in a 200-entry\n  local array and moves them to malloc()ed memory (YYSTACK_ALLOC) once\n  the input nests deeper; it frees them only when it returns, and the\n  error long-jumps past that. The comment on the same leak in\n  src/main/gram.y notes that \"The same problem exists at least in the\n  Rd and LaTeX parsers in tools.\"\n- PUSH_BACK() grows 'pushbase' with malloc() for long macro\n  expansions; ParseRd() frees it only on its normal return, and the\n  next parse resets 'pushbase' to the static 'pushback' array, losing\n  the grown buffer.\n\nMeasurements: aarch64-apple-darwin25.6.0, macOS leaks after the loop\nabove (1000 failing calls):\n\n    unpatched trunk r90590:  993 leaks for 97648640 total leaked bytes.\n    patched:                 0 leaks for 0 total leaked bytes.\n\nThis was found with LeakSanitizer by a new parse_Rd() fuzz target in\nhttps://github.com/r-devel/r-oss-fuzz; the defect itself is independent\nof fuzzing.\n\nProposed patch: attached as parse-rd-error-leaks.diff. It defines\nYYMALLOC/YYFREE in the gramRd.y prologue as wrappers that record the\nblocks bison has live in a small table, and adds FreeParseBuffers(),\nwhich frees whatever is still recorded plus a grown pushback buffer,\nand resets the pushback state. ParseRd() calls it in place of the old\npushback free, and PopStateOnError() -- the cleanup added for PR#19121,\nalready the cend of the context parseRd() sets up around each parse --\ncalls it on the error path.\n\nThe table has three entries rather than recording only the newest\nblock because bison also allocates long verbose syntax-error messages\nthrough YYSTACK_ALLOC, and the Rd grammar recovers from syntax errors,\nso a message buffer and a grown stack can both be live when a later\nerror unwinds (as can the newline warning in yyerror() under\noptions(warn = 2)).\n\ngramRd.c is regenerated with bison 3.8.2, which reproduces the\nchecked-in file byte for byte from the unpatched gramRd.y; apart from\nthe prologue and epilogue, the only change is the yyrline debug table,\nwhose line numbers shift. All 1489 .Rd files under src/library parse\nto identical results, as do several malformed fragments (error\nmessages included), and make test-Reg passes.\n\nThe patch is also submitted as https://github.com/r-devel/r-svn/pull/344,\nwhere CI results can be seen.",
            "bug_id": 19184
